Z||000012||MrFeeArrow||06-01-2001||07:46 AM||||Thanks for the info guy's,.....by the way I negelected to let everyone know that I had previously last summer reto-fitted to the 134a with the kits you can buy in the stores. My system was dead empty and I re-filled to 80%,....everything was fine until I got greedy and put more freon in,....NOT GOOD!! there is a reason the system should not go above 80%,....well needless to day I blew my compressor.<P>Another note: The air worked fine when above idle,....but at idle the cooling dropped off considerably. I was told that I needed a variable orifice at the condenser to remedy this problem using the 134a. <P>Anyone have the same results?||||reg||14
Z||000013||thomas_l||06-01-2001||09:39 AM||||There's really four things that are issues when going from R12 to R134a:<P>1. The lube is incompatible. R12 systems use mineral oil and R134a systems don't. The a/c system relies on the lube being carried thru by the refrigerant. Mineral oil in an R134a system finds low spots and "pools" instead of getting picked up by the refrigerant. Small amounts of mineral oil can be left in the system; however, every bit of mineral oil displaces potential space for more refrigerant.<P>2. The molecular size of R134a is 6 times smaller than R12. Because of that, R134a systems use "barrier" hoses. These hoses are lined with a material that R134a can't penetrate. Most cars built after '92-93 are equipped with barrier hoses even when OEM charged with R12. Yes that means our Fieros have the old rubber hoses that R134a will seep through.<P>3. R134a attacks the old style rubber o-rings. That's why even the cheap parts store conversion kits come with new o-rings.<P>4. R134a has a slightly higher vapor pressure than R12. A/c units operate by the temperature change associated with a gas going to liquid state and vice-versa. R134a has more high-side pressure to achieve the same cooling effect as R12 (in other words, it's less efficient than R12). This results in most kits and/or compressors coming with different switch calibrations. The variable orfice tube is another method of improving R134a cooling as there is greater control over how much evaporation occurs at different engine (compressor) speeds.<P>The short version is that you can do 50% (get by) job that will last a season, best case, by using an over the counter conversion kit and just filling your system with R134a. You can do a 95% job by getting all your hose assemblies changed out to barrier hose, new o-rings, a new compressor fitted for R134a, and new pressure switches and variable orfice tube. I have been thinking of this myself and figure I'll have somewhere around $300 in parts to do it myself + I'll have to buy a set of R134a manifold gauges. If Auto Air of GA will do it for $600, that's not a bad deal. At least that way if you pay them, you can have them do it over and over until it works right  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Raydar||06-01-2001||10:15 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by thomas_l:<BR><B>If Auto Air of GA will do it for $600, that's not a bad deal. At least that way if you pay them, you can have them do it over and over until it works right    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thomas, the $600 is for a 92 S-10, not a Fiero. I didn't realize the hoses were different, but hopefully mine are the later version. <BR>The compressor locked up, but I think the system is still sealed. Don't know what kind of debris the compressor might have puked into the lines, though.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>I'd try repair/convert it myself, except I don't have any way to recover the R-12, and don't particularly want to just dump it. I also don't have the means to vacuum the system. (It does have to be vacuumed. right?)<BR>I'm sure the Fiero would be more expensive, due to the "fun-to-get-to-everything" factor, if nothing else. <BR>But not $1800.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 06-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||SteveJ||06-01-2001||10:23 AM||||OK so where do we come by a vacumn pump at a reasonable price for casual use?||||reg||1
Z||000016||Formula88||06-01-2001||10:31 AM||||Just a couple of points.  (I used to do A/C work).  A/C isn't rocket science, but a few precautions must be heeded.  Make sure you don't mix R12 and 134a.  They aren't compatible.  If your system has bled empty, no problem, but you'll still need to pull a vacuum on it and add fresh oil.  Without pulling a vacuum, any air in the system will displace refrigerant and you'll never get the right charge.  Also, yes this is common sense to most people, but... be very careful when bleeding your system!  Freon (R12 or 134a) can cause severe frostbite INSTANTLY if it comes in contact with your skin.  I had a fitting break off in my hand once, and my entire hand got 1st & 2nd degree burns from frostbite just in the time it took me to jerk my hand back.  Be careful.  <P>Oh, and don't forget, it's a Federal crime to vent R12 into the atmosphere.  That's why shops charge so much to recover it.  They have to account for all of it, and have special equipment for recovery.  When I was studying HVAC in technical school - we just opened the valve and let it vent.  Doing that today would land you in jail!||||reg||6
Z||000017||MrFeeArrow||06-01-2001||12:05 PM||||Well after getting estimates from just about everyone here in North East Florida I decided to go for the 134a conversion.<P>All shops were pretty much the same,....anywhere from $750 - $1200,....I had mine done this morning at All Pro, they replaced the pump(blown), accumulator, new vairable orifice, flushed and vacuumed system,and refill.<P>Total damage.....$1033.16,....90 day warranty on everything. It's as cold as I can remember it being with the old R-12.<P>I let you guy's know how it fairs in the long run,....so far so good.||||reg||12
Z||000018||theogre||06-01-2001||01:16 PM||||Arrow, A grand for that much work isn't bad. You'd have to do all that no matter which refrigerant you used due to the blown compressor.<P>I'm not sure where some of the above info came from. I don't think I want to know...<P>After 15-20 years, The ACC/Dryer is shot. The thing was only built to last about 7 years and doesn't have the correct dessicant for R134. The thing may work when a R134 conversion is dumped into it but don't be suprised if the desicant bag fails and takes the compressor with it. This thing is cheap, about $75us, and can be changed with common tools while the system is empty. It's just not worth the trouble to leave it or the orifice tube, $6-7us, in place.<P>Left over mineral oil can and usually will pool in low places. Including the compressor itself. This very often will interfere with the free flow of the new oil. You hope most of it ends up in other places... The old A6 compressors will often tolerate this but the V5's really don't like an over oiled system. Fortunatly for most people here, the V5 is used only in 87-88 4 cyl fiero.<P>Variuous testing of used hoses has shown interesting results. The original tests of R134 and old style hose were done with fresh hose. There has been more testing of used systems to see how the used hose handles a conversion. This testing has shown that the lubricants soak into the hose and seal most of the pours that would let R134 thru fresh hose. This means that used hose is much better at handling R134 than originally thought.<P>As mentioned above, Profesional removal of R12 is required by law in the U.S. and many other countires. Pulling and holding a full vacuum is an absolute must with a R134 conversion. The 2 refrigerants Do Not play well together and can cause the system to self destruct. Pulling a vacuum and holding it also removes air and boils water out of the system. Small amounts of water can be big problems. (Very often freezes in the orifice tube and causes excessive high side pressure... that causes problems including compressor failure)<P>If the system has leaked down, FIND OUT WHY! Don't keep stuffing more refrigerant in it. It's not hard to find a leak. Squirt some dry in there and watch it with a UV light. You can get dye injectors at many parts stores and even wall/K mart stores allot of the time. Some dyes even work in normal light. The dye will show the leak in fairly short order. The only part of the system you can't get to easily for dye testing is the evaporator but everything else is accessable. (You might need a jack to check a few areas.) The leak is letting water into the system as well as letting out refrigerant. Even fresh desicant will only absorb so much water. the rest is free to trash the system.||||reg||1
Z||000019||thomas_l||06-01-2001||02:06 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>I'm not sure where some of the above info came from. I don't think I want to know....</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>10 years of experience as a professional. That being said, I left the automotive career for easier work and more money in '97 - so there is likely more info someone still in the field can add and/or correct me on. Especially in R134a conversions as that was still a fairly new thing when I quit working on cars for a living. That bit about the used hoses is interesting and makes a lot of sense. As far as dye being visible on the evaporator - just watch for bright green water dripping out of the evap case  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">. Here in the humid SE the evaporators have lots of condensation dripping out in short order so it probably shows a little quicker than in some drier climates.<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>  Thomas, the $600 is for a 92 S-10, not a Fiero.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, but you really aren't talking much difference labor-wise unless a dishonest service dealer is trying to smoke you with scary accessibility excuses. OK - maybe add a little time for the compressor, but all the rest is not hard to get to in a shop equipped with a lift. I agree 100% with theogre's parts list and pricing will vary by location and variations on that list of parts. Some shops may even add stuff (evaporator, condensor, etc) as a shotgun to insure that the job doesn't come back. The exception as you noted was referred to as "black death". This occurs when the dessicant bag inside the accumulator lets go and it's guts clog everything up solid. In that case you are likely facing 100% system replacement and can easily go over $1000. The $600 amount was in line with a conversion to an otherwise solid but old R12 system with maybe seeping o-rings or compressor shaft seal, not catastrophic damage.<P>I'm not saying it's impossible to do at home. You can pay for an "a/c service" at most quick-lubes and get them to recover your R12 to avoid venting it, and maybe even pump R134a back in on a return trip when you're finished hanging parts. But for most folks, by the time you buy a vacuum pump, service gauges, and other miscellaneous crap, you don't save much unless your working on a lot of a/c systems.<p>[This message has been edited by thomas_l (edited 06-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||Raydar||06-01-2001||03:04 PM||||Okay... I'm listening and trying to learn. How likely is it that my compressor failed <I>because</I> the dessicant bag failed? Seems like it got noisy, started clattering, a few days before it locked up. A/C performance never changed right up until I heard the belt squealing, although I never thought it worked as good as a typical GM system ought to. (Bought the truck new.) I always attributed the perceived lack of performance to having a black truck. <BR>I'm still not really leaning toward doing this myself, but I'd just like to know what to expect when the shop dives in to it.<BR>BTW... That price includes a new compressor, new accumulator, and the R-134 conversion.<BR>Thanks for all the info.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 06-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||p4n1c||06-01-2001||03:12 PM||||To get my car done it's going to be $600 Canadian.  They screwed up the first time doing it, by not putting on the right seals and the refridgerant leaked out through the old ones.  And it's been about 5 years since it's had working A/C.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International<BR>2.8L MPFI V6/TH440-T4 OD auto<BR>"Ride the geriatric mobile.. err..  rocket"||||reg||1
Z||000022||MrFeeArrow||06-01-2001||03:44 PM||||OK,...here's the break down from the reciept for all parts and labor.<P>1.Compressor...............394.00<BR>2.Accumulator..............125.93<BR>3.Orifice..................  8.38<BR>4.Flush.................... 60.00<BR>5.Evac&Recharge............ 84.00<BR>6.Freon 2.5 lbs............ 75.00<BR>7.Labor....................210.00<BR>8.TAX MAN.................. 67.85<BR>  Total...................1033.68<P>This seems to be the going rate for our area.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000023||thomas_l||06-01-2001||03:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>How likely is it that my compressor failed <I>because</I> the dessicant bag failed?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>OK - it probably died as a result of lack of lubrication, and probably from a low R12 charge. I don't remember the exact numbers, but I read somewhere that lubrication effectiveness falls off at over double the rate of refrigerant loss. For example, a system with 80% R12 charge loses half 50%! of it's lube capability. This is because the oil is circulated by the refrigerant. If your compressor started chattering, got worse, and finally locked, it was probably running low and starved for oil. And it probably did spit some junk into the system, but nothing like a dessicant bag letting go. The compressor debris (not much usually) should come out with a good flush. Dessicant debris is like trying to flush plaster out of a mold after it sets up. Your repair shop will know what's up as soon as they evac the remaining R12 and open some lines. If the dessicant bag popped, the pressure line and most of the condensor will be full of black powdery crap. If that happens, they are going to replace everything. I have seen many attempts to flush & shortcut and they always come back with a clogged orfice tube.<P>Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000024||thomas_l||06-01-2001||04:07 PM||||YOW! No wonder your price seemed a bit high. They must have sold you a new instead of reman compressor. Also, $30/lb for R134a??? Maybe I'm dated but I just saw 30 lb bottles for around $102 + shipping. OK so if 30 lb cost $150 that would be $5/lb. quite a nice markup to get $30/lb retail (FYI: same wholesaler had R12 at $29.16/lb). Maybe they are charging that to get back the cost of the recovery machine? Or was that included in the evac & recharge fee? Or was that labor included in the labor charge? Now my head is hurting so I need to stop  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> The rest looks about average.<P>Am I being f***ing cheap? I know you didn't go to Goodwill for this repair and I'm not used to paying the Man to do my repairs, but WOW.||||reg||1
Z||000025||MrFeeArrow||06-01-2001||04:20 PM||||Now for the results of 134a conversion:<BR>Test drive #1<BR>Ambient temp. 90 degrees and clear,....very cold in max, can notice the clutch cycle every 15 seconds or so. Selecting HI the clutch does not cylce at all and the air is still very cool<P>Test drive #2<BR>Ambient temp. 73 degrees with thunderstorms....still verycold in max with clutch cycling as before. Selecting HI is still very cold with same clutch cycles, selecting low and max has same results.<P>Note: The clutch stays engaged and never cycles while at idle.<P>Overall this conversion seems to be a success, the only question I have is the cycling of the clutch. It would seem that the system is low on freon and that the low pressure switch is doing it's job? Or that the system under cooler outside ambient temps compensates this way?<P>What do you say experts? <BR>||||reg||1
